#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wf-model-budget — emit the model budget picker's options, mechanically.

`audit` Step 0.4a and `model-map` render FIVE lane objects — `analytical · Lead`,
`analytical · IC`, `creative-text`, `creative-visual`, `code` — each offering the
four static models in cost order with the lane's recommended model marked
`(recommended)`. Until now that option set was RECONSTRUCTED from prose by the
running agent on every run, and it drifted.

FIVE LANES DO NOT FIT ONE CALL. `AskUserQuestion` accepts at most FOUR objects per
call (`platform.md` § header), so the five lanes render as TWO calls and this script
emits that grouping explicitly rather than leaving the split to the caller: CALL A
carries the three structural lanes (`analytical · Lead`, `analytical · IC`, `code`)
and CALL B carries the two creative lanes (`creative-text`, `creative-visual`). A
caller that regroups them by hand is reintroducing by hand the drift this script
exists to foreclose.

ONE ROW MAY BE RECOMMENDED FOR SEVERAL LANES. Since 2026-09-02 the top pool row is
recommended for both `code` and `creative-visual`, so every `Recommended for` annotation
a row carries is read: a lane is never recommended twice, but a model may be
recommended for as many lanes as its Notes cell names.

ONE SANCTIONED OUT-OF-POOL RECOMMENDATION. `creative-visual` is the one lane that MAY
be recommended a model that is DELIBERATELY not a § Model statics row (as it was with
`claude-fable-5` until 2026-09-02, when that model's successor entered the pool as row 1
and the shipped template stopped exercising this path). When a lane is, its
recommendation is annotated on a PROSE line inside § Model statics rather than on a
table row. It is nonetheless OFFERED THE SAME WAY every other lane's recommendation
is offered: it LEADS that lane's slate as a first-class option, marked
`(recommended)` in place and `[pre-selected]` when it is the resolved default. A
recommendation the user can only reach by hand-typing it is a recommendation the
question does not actually make. That costs one option — the four pool rows already
fill `AskUserQuestion`'s cap — so exactly enough pool models are dropped FROM THAT
LANE'S SLATE ONLY to fit, taken positionally from the cheap end of the cost order and
never by ID, and never the lane's RECORDED default (a re-audit's own recorded choice
must not vanish from the slate it is pre-selected in). The global pool line and every
other lane still carry all four statics. The `+ Other` line reverts to a plain
free-text catch-all for this lane; a recorded out-of-pool "Other" PIN still rides it,
named, so neither reads as a static that went missing. Exactly ONE lane may be
recommended out of pool; a second one is an ERROR (exit 2), because a recommendation
that quietly resolves outside the pool is indistinguishable from the pool having gone
stale — the very confusion this script was written to end.

WHY THIS EXISTS — a measured, recurring drift.

`references/org-config.template.md` § Model statics is the SOLE source of the four
statics (`audit-setup.md` § Step 0.4a: "read from the shipped template's § Model
statics and from nowhere else"). A project's instantiated `org-config.md` records
chosen values, not the pool. But an older instantiation copied a `## Model statics`
section INTO the project file, and on a re-audit the agent reconstructing the picker
read that co-located stale copy instead of the template — proposing the
pre-`claude-sonnet-5` four (`claude-fable-5` present as a static, `claude-sonnet-5`
absent) and marking `claude-opus-4-8` as the undifferentiated "analytical"
recommendation instead of splitting Lead/IC. The template names this exact failure
twice (§ Model statics line 34, `audit-setup.md` line 393) and guarded it with PROSE
ONLY, so it recurred: the picker showed the stale four on precisely the projects that
carry the stale section — re-audits that predate the sonnet-5 split — and the right
four elsewhere. "Not consistently across all projects" is the signature of a
prose-only rule that bites only the contaminated inputs.

A DETECTOR SHIPS WITH ITS FIX (`SKILL.md` § Directives, 2026-08-05). The fix is to
stop reconstructing the option set and DERIVE it, so the picker is a pure function of
the template and cannot be contaminated by anything a project file carries.

WHAT IT DERIVES, and from where:
  * The four statics, in the template's cost order, with their Notes — parsed from
    § Model statics. No model ID is written in this script; "never fabricate a model
    ID" (`SKILL.md` Core Principle 5) and "constants stated once" (Principle 9a) both
    forbid a second copy of the pool living in code.
  * The per-lane recommendation — parsed from each Notes cell's `Recommended for
    <lane>` annotation, so re-splitting or re-pointing a recommendation is a
    template edit with no code change (`org-config.template.md` § Model statics:
    "the budget question reads these cells and has no other source").
  * The ONE sanctioned out-of-pool recommendation, when the template carries one: the
    same `Recommended for <lane>` annotation, found on a PROSE line of § Model statics
    beside a backticked model ID that is not a pool row. `claude-fable-5` reached
    `creative-visual` this way until 2026-09-02, and no model ID for it was ever
    written here either.
  * The question HEADER and BODY per call — parsed from § Budget question wording,
    the one home of that copy, so this script prints the WHOLE call and the caller
    renders it verbatim. An option set produced mechanically under a header retyped
    from memory is still half a hand-built question, and the reconstruction hole was
    left open on that half until 2026-08-26.
  * The pre-selected default per lane — the recorded value from the project's
    `org-config.md` four-lane tables when one is passed (a re-audit), ELSE the lane's
    recommended model (a first run defaults to the recommendation, `audit-setup.md`
    § Step 0.4a). A recorded value that is not one of the four statics is still
    honored as the default and flagged, because a project may pin an "Other" model.

WHAT IT REFUSES: the pool is NEVER read from `--config`. If the project's
`org-config.md` carries its own `## Model statics` heading, it is reported as a stale
legacy artifact and read PAST, never clobbered (the project file is the user's,
`org-config.template.md` § Model statics: the never-clobber rule holds).

The recommendation is a LABEL, never a POSITION (`org-config.template.md` § Model
statics): POOL options are emitted in cost order and `(recommended)` is appended in
place, never promoted to the top. This script emits them in that order and marks in
place; a caller that reorders them is reintroducing the drift by hand. The rule binds
where the cost order HAS a position to respect. The out-of-pool recommendation is not
a table row and has no rank in that order, so leading its lane's slate promotes it
past nothing — the pool rows below it are still in cost order, still marked in place.

EXIT CODES: 0 normal (including when a stale embedded section was reported — it is a
finding to surface, not a failure to abort on); 2 when `--root` is empty, when the
template's § Model statics or § Budget question wording cannot be found or parsed, or when a lane other than the
one sanctioned lane resolves to an out-of-pool recommendation — in each of those the
caller has no trustworthy pool and must not fall back to reconstructing one.
"""

import argparse
import os
import re
import sys

# The canonical lane order, owned by org-config.template.md § The four lanes and
# restated in neither budget. Parsed labels from the Notes column map onto these.
# The lane KEY is the token the annotation is written with and the token the two
# budgets agree on; the second field is the object header, the third its prompt.
LANES = [
    ("analytical-lead", "analytical · Lead", "Agents that coordinate"),
    ("analytical-ic", "analytical · IC", "Agents that do the work"),
    ("creative-text", "creative-text", "Writing & copy work"),
    ("creative-visual", "creative-visual", "Graphics & frontend design work"),
    ("code", "code", "Code work"),
]

# AskUserQuestion accepts at most this many objects per call (platform.md § header).
_OBJECT_CAP = 4

# ...and at most this many options within one object. Aliased rather than restated so
# the number lives in exactly one place in this file: a cap refresh is one edit, not a
# hunt for the second copy (SKILL.md Principle 9a). Only a lane whose recommendation
# sits outside the pool can reach this cap — see _lane_slate().
_OPTION_CAP = _OBJECT_CAP

# Five lanes overflow one call, so the picker renders as two. The grouping lives
# here, next to the lane order it partitions, so the two cannot drift apart: the
# validation below refuses a grouping that drops, duplicates, or overfills a lane.
CALL_GROUPS = [
    ("CALL A", "structural lanes", ("analytical-lead", "analytical-ic", "code")),
    ("CALL B", "creative lanes", ("creative-text", "creative-visual")),
]

# EXACTLY ONE lane may be recommended a model that is not in the pool. That lane
# OFFERS it first-class — leading its slate, marked in place — and the emission names
# it as the sanctioned out, so it never reads as a static that went missing. Any
# OTHER lane resolving out of pool is an error, not a silent acceptance — see
# merge_recommendations().
SANCTIONED_OUT_OF_POOL_LANES = ("creative-visual",)

# Maps the phrase in a "**Recommended for X**" annotation to a lane key above.
# Order matters: "analytical IC"/"analytical Lead" must be tested before a bare
# "analytical" would ever match, so the two-word forms are the only ones listed.
# The trailing `(?![\w-])` is what keeps the two hyphenated creative lanes apart:
# without it a `creative-text` pattern would swallow `creative-visual`'s annotation
# (and a bare `creative`, a lane that no longer exists, would swallow both).
_REC_PATTERNS = [
    (re.compile(r"Recommended for analytical Lead(?![\w-])", re.I), "analytical-lead"),
    (re.compile(r"Recommended for analytical IC(?![\w-])", re.I), "analytical-ic"),
    (re.compile(r"Recommended for creative-text(?![\w-])", re.I), "creative-text"),
    (re.compile(r"Recommended for creative-visual(?![\w-])", re.I), "creative-visual"),
    (re.compile(r"Recommended for code(?![\w-])", re.I), "code"),
]

# A statics row looks like: | 1 | `<model-id>` | 1M | 128K | ...notes... |
_POOL_ROW_RE = re.compile(r"^\|\s*\d+\s*\|\s*`([^`]+)`\s*\|.*\|.*\|\s*(.*?)\s*\|\s*$")

# The `### Wording: <budget> / CALL <letter>` sub-headings of § Budget question
# wording. wf-effort-budget reads the same section with the same shape — the two
# budgets share one home for their copy, which is the point of moving it there.
_WORDING_HEAD_RE = re.compile(r"^###\s+Wording:\s*([A-Za-z-]+)\s*/\s*CALL\s+([A-Z])\s*$")

_LANE_KEYS = [k for k, _lbl, _p in LANES]


def _die(msg, code=2):
    sys.stderr.write("wf-model-budget: " + msg + "\n")
    sys.exit(code)


def _validate_grouping():
    """The call grouping must partition the lanes exactly and fit the tool's cap.

    A lane silently missing from both calls is a lane the user is never asked
    about, and the run would pin it to a default nobody chose."""
    grouped = [k for _n, _d, keys in CALL_GROUPS for k in keys]
    if sorted(grouped) != sorted(_LANE_KEYS):
        _die("internal: CALL_GROUPS does not partition LANES (%s vs %s)"
             % (", ".join(sorted(grouped)), ", ".join(sorted(_LANE_KEYS))))
    for name, _desc, keys in CALL_GROUPS:
        if len(keys) > _OBJECT_CAP:
            _die("internal: %s carries %d objects; the cap is %d per call"
                 % (name, len(keys), _OBJECT_CAP))


def _read(path):
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            return fh.read()
    except OSError as exc:
        _die("cannot read %s: %s" % (path, exc))


def default_template_path():
    """The shipped template co-located with this script: bin/ and references/ are
    siblings in all three copies (source, runtime, personal install)."""
    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
    return os.path.normpath(os.path.join(here, "..", "references", "org-config.template.md"))


def _section(text, heading_re):
    """Return the body between a heading matching heading_re and the next `## `."""
    lines = text.splitlines()
    out, capturing = [], False
    for line in lines:
        if capturing:
            if line.startswith("## "):
                break
            out.append(line)
        elif heading_re.match(line):
            capturing = True
    return "\n".join(out) if capturing else None


def parse_statics(template_text):
    """Parse § Model statics into an ordered list of (model_id, notes, [rec_lanes]).

    Cost order is table order — the template states it is ordered most-expensive-first
    and presented in that order every time, so this function never re-sorts."""
    body = _statics_body(template_text)
    rows = []
    for line in body.splitlines():
        m = _POOL_ROW_RE.match(line)
        if not m:
            continue
        model_id, notes = m.group(1).strip(), m.group(2).strip()
        rows.append((model_id, notes, _rec_lanes(notes)))
    if not rows:
        _die("'## Model statics' present but no model rows parsed: " + "table shape changed")
    return rows


def _statics_body(template_text):
    body = _section(template_text, re.compile(r"^##\s+Model statics"))
    if body is None:
        _die("template has no '## Model statics' section: " + "cannot derive the pool")
    return body


def _rec_lanes(text):
    """Every lane a `**Recommended for <lane>**` annotation on this text names.

    One row may carry several (since 2026-09-02 the top row is recommended for both
    `code` and `creative-visual`), so this returns all of them, in canonical lane
    order, and an empty list when the text carries none."""
    return [lane for pat, lane in _REC_PATTERNS if pat.search(text)]


def parse_out_of_pool_recs(template_text, pool_ids):
    """Lane recommendations annotated on a NON-pool model ID.

    Read from the same § Model statics section, but from its PROSE lines: a model
    that is deliberately not a static has no table row to carry its Notes cell, so
    its annotation sits beside the backticked ID in prose. A prose
    line that names only pool IDs is documentation restating the table and is
    skipped. Returns {lane_key: model_id}."""
    body = _statics_body(template_text)
    found = {}
    for line in body.splitlines():
        if _POOL_ROW_RE.match(line):
            continue  # a pool row — parse_statics owns it
        lanes = _rec_lanes(line)
        if not lanes:
            continue
        outside = [t.strip() for t in re.findall(r"`([^`]+)`", line)
                   if t.strip() and t.strip() not in pool_ids]
        if not outside:
            continue
        model_id = outside[0]
        for lane in lanes:
            if found.get(lane, model_id) != model_id:
                _die("lane '%s' is annotated with two different out-of-pool models "
                     "('%s' and '%s') in § Model statics: the recommendation is ambiguous"
                     % (lane, found[lane], model_id))
            found[lane] = model_id
    return found


def parse_wording(template_text):
    """{(budget, call_letter): (header_line, [body_lines])} from § Budget question
    wording — the ONE home of the four calls' user-facing copy.

    The blockquote's first line is the question header and every line after it is the
    body, blank lines included."""
    body = _section(template_text, re.compile(r"^##\s+Budget question wording"))
    if body is None:
        _die("template has no '## Budget question wording' section: the caller would "
             "reconstruct the question text by hand, which is the drift this emitter "
             "exists to end")
    found, key, quoted = {}, None, []

    def _close():
        if key and quoted:
            found[key] = (quoted[0], quoted[1:])

    for line in body.splitlines():
        m = _WORDING_HEAD_RE.match(line)
        if m:
            _close()
            key, quoted = (m.group(1).strip().lower(), m.group(2).strip().upper()), []
        elif line.startswith(">") and key:
            quoted.append(line[1:].lstrip(" ") if line[1:2] == " " else line[1:])
        elif line.strip() and key and quoted:
            _close()
            key, quoted = None, []
    _close()
    return found


def wording_for(wording, budget, call_name):
    letter = call_name.split()[-1]
    got = wording.get((budget, letter))
    if got is None:
        _die("no wording block for '%s / %s' in '## Budget question wording': every "
             "call renders header, body, and options together or the caller retypes "
             "the half that is missing" % (budget, call_name))
    return got


def recommended_by_lane(statics):
    rec = {}
    for model_id, _notes, lanes in statics:
        for lane in lanes:
            rec[lane] = model_id
    return rec


def merge_recommendations(statics, out_of_pool):
    """Fold the out-of-pool annotations into the in-pool ones, TIGHTLY.

    Exactly one lane is sanctioned to be recommended a model outside the pool. A
    second one is refused rather than accepted, because an unexplained out-of-pool
    recommendation and a pool that has gone stale look identical in the emission —
    and reading a stale pool is the failure this script was written to end."""
    rec = recommended_by_lane(statics)
    for lane in sorted(out_of_pool):
        model_id = out_of_pool[lane]
        if lane not in SANCTIONED_OUT_OF_POOL_LANES:
            _die("unsanctioned out-of-pool recommendation: lane '%s' is recommended "
                 "'%s', which is not a row of the template's § Model statics table. "
                 "Exactly one lane may be recommended out of pool (%s); every other "
                 "lane's recommendation must come from a pool table row."
                 % (lane, model_id, ", ".join(SANCTIONED_OUT_OF_POOL_LANES)))
        if rec.get(lane, model_id) != model_id:
            _die("lane '%s' is recommended twice: '%s' by a pool table row and '%s' "
                 "by an out-of-pool annotation in § Model statics"
                 % (lane, rec[lane], model_id))
        rec[lane] = model_id
    return rec


# --- project config parsing (re-audit defaults only; NEVER the pool) -------------

def _find_backticked(text, label_re):
    """First backticked token on a table row whose first cell matches label_re."""
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if not line.lstrip().startswith("|"):
            continue
        cells = [c.strip() for c in line.strip().strip("|").split("|")]
        if not cells:
            continue
        if label_re.search(cells[0]):
            for cell in cells[1:]:
                bt = re.search(r"`([^`]+)`", cell)
                if bt:
                    return bt.group(1).strip()
    return None


def parse_config_defaults(config_text):
    """Recorded per-lane models from a project's org-config.md lane tables.
    Best-effort: a lane that cannot be found simply has no recorded default and
    falls back to the recommendation. Returns {lane_key: model_id}.

    A config written before the creative lane split records one `Creative model`
    row and no visual row. That row is the recorded TEXT model — reading it as
    nothing would silently discard a choice the user made, so it is honoured as the
    `creative-text` default when no `Creative-text model` row is present. The visual
    lane then simply has no recorded default and first-runs on its recommendation,
    which is the truth about such a config: it predates the lane."""
    defaults = {}
    lead = _find_backticked(config_text, re.compile(r"^Lead\b", re.I))
    ic = _find_backticked(config_text, re.compile(r"^IC\b", re.I))
    ctext = _find_backticked(config_text, re.compile(r"^Creative-text model\b", re.I))
    if not ctext:
        ctext = _find_backticked(config_text, re.compile(r"^Creative model\b", re.I))
    cvisual = _find_backticked(config_text, re.compile(r"^Creative-visual model\b", re.I))
    code = _find_backticked(config_text, re.compile(r"^Code model\b", re.I))
    if lead:
        defaults["analytical-lead"] = lead
    if ic:
        defaults["analytical-ic"] = ic
    if ctext:
        defaults["creative-text"] = ctext
    if cvisual:
        defaults["creative-visual"] = cvisual
    if code:
        defaults["code"] = code
    return defaults


def config_has_stale_statics(config_text):
    return bool(re.search(r"^##\s+Model statics", config_text, re.M))


# --- emission --------------------------------------------------------------------

def _lane_slate(statics, rec_model, recorded_default, pool_ids):
    """The models THIS lane offers, in emission order, and the ones it gave up.

    A lane whose recommendation sits in the pool offers the whole pool, in cost
    order, unchanged — this returns it untouched and gives up nothing.

    A lane whose recommendation sits OUTSIDE the pool offers it first-class, at the
    head of the slate: a recommendation the user can only reach by hand-typing it is
    a recommendation the question does not actually make. That costs one option and
    the pool rows already fill the option cap, so enough pool models are dropped FROM
    THIS SLATE ONLY to fit. The drop is POSITIONAL — the cost order is
    most-expensive-first, so the walk starts at the last element, the cheapest — and
    never by ID: naming the model to drop would put a second copy of the pool in this
    file, which is the drift the whole script exists to foreclose.

    The lane's RECORDED default is never the model dropped. A re-audit that recorded
    the cheapest pool model would otherwise watch its own recorded choice disappear
    from the slate it is pre-selected in, which is the silent discard this project
    keeps finding; the walk skips it and takes the next-cheapest instead."""
    pool = [m for m, _n, _l in statics]
    if rec_model is None or rec_model in pool_ids:
        return pool, []
    slate, dropped, i = [rec_model] + pool, [], len(pool)
    while len(slate) > _OPTION_CAP and i > 0:
        if slate[i] != recorded_default:
            dropped.append(slate.pop(i))
        i -= 1
    return slate, dropped


def _lane_block(lane_key, lane_label, prompt_label, statics, rec, defaults, pool_ids):
    """One AskUserQuestion object, rendered. Pool options are emitted in cost order
    and the marks are appended IN PLACE — a recommendation is a label, never a
    position. An out-of-pool recommendation has no rank in that order to respect, so
    it leads the slate; see _lane_slate() for what that costs and who pays it."""
    out = []
    rec_model = rec.get(lane_key)
    default_model = defaults.get(lane_key, rec_model)
    slate, dropped = _lane_slate(statics, rec_model, defaults.get(lane_key), pool_ids)
    out.append("LANE %s  (object: \"%s\")" % (lane_label, prompt_label))
    if rec_model is None:
        out.append("  !! no model is marked 'Recommended for %s' in the template" % lane_key)
    for model_id in slate:
        marks = []
        if model_id == rec_model:
            marks.append("(recommended)")
        if model_id == default_model:
            marks.append("[pre-selected]")
        suffix = ("  " + " ".join(marks)) if marks else ""
        out.append("  - %s%s" % (model_id, suffix))

    # The Other line is a PLAIN free-text catch-all: this lane's recommendation, in
    # or out of pool, is an option above and is not pre-filled here. One thing still
    # rides it — a recorded "Other" pin the user hand-typed on a previous run, which
    # is a recorded choice and not a recommendation. It is named, so it never reads
    # as a static that went missing.
    other_pin = None
    if (default_model is not None and default_model not in pool_ids
            and default_model != rec_model):
        other_pin = default_model
    if other_pin is not None:
        out.append("  + Other (hand-typed model ID) = %s  [pre-selected]" % other_pin)
    else:
        out.append("  + Other (hand-typed model ID)")

    if rec_model is not None and rec_model not in pool_ids:
        out.append("    out-of-pool: %s is this lane's recommended model and is NOT a "
                   "§ Model statics row. It was not dropped from the pool — it LEADS "
                   "this lane's options as a first-class pick, marked in place, and is "
                   "not something to hand-type (the one sanctioned out-of-pool "
                   "recommendation, lane %s). Dropped from THIS lane's slate only, to "
                   "fit the %d-option cap: %s — the cheapest pool model this lane can "
                   "spare, never its recorded default. The global pool line above is "
                   "unchanged and every other lane still offers all of it."
                   % (rec_model, lane_key, _OPTION_CAP,
                      ", ".join(dropped) if dropped else "none"))
    if (lane_key in defaults and default_model is not None
            and default_model not in pool_ids and default_model != rec_model):
        out.append("    pre-selected = %s (recorded 'Other' pin, not a static)" % default_model)
    src = "recorded (re-audit)" if lane_key in defaults else "recommended default (first run)"
    out.append("  default source: %s" % src)
    out.append("")
    return out


def emit(statics, rec, defaults, stale, template_path, wording):
    pool_ids = [m for m, _n, _l in statics]
    pool_set = set(pool_ids)
    meta = {k: (lbl, prompt) for k, lbl, prompt in LANES}
    out = []
    out.append("WF-MODEL-BUDGET  (Step 0.4a option set — render verbatim, do not reorder)")
    out.append("source: %s § Model statics" % template_path)
    out.append("wording source: %s § Budget question wording" % template_path)
    out.append("pool (cost order, most expensive first): " + ", ".join(pool_ids))
    out.append("lanes: %d, rendered as %d AskUserQuestion calls (cap: %d objects per call)"
               % (len(LANES), len(CALL_GROUPS), _OBJECT_CAP))
    out.append("")
    for call_name, call_desc, keys in CALL_GROUPS:
        ordered = [k for k in _LANE_KEYS if k in keys]
        out.append("%s  (%s — %d objects: %s)"
                   % (call_name, call_desc, len(ordered),
                      ", ".join(meta[k][0] for k in ordered)))
        header, body = wording_for(wording, "model", call_name)
        out.append("QUESTION-HEADER-BEGIN")
        out.append(header)
        out.append("QUESTION-HEADER-END")
        out.append("QUESTION-BODY-BEGIN")
        out.extend(body)
        out.append("QUESTION-BODY-END")
        out.append("")
        for lane_key in ordered:
            lane_label, prompt_label = meta[lane_key]
            out.extend(_lane_block(lane_key, lane_label, prompt_label,
                                   statics, rec, defaults, pool_set))
    if stale:
        out.append("STALE-ARTIFACT: the project's org-config.md carries a '## Model statics' "
                   "heading. The pool was read from the template, never from that copy. "
                   "Report it as a stale legacy artifact; never rewrite or delete it "
                   "(org-config.template.md § Model statics — the never-clobber rule holds).")
    else:
        out.append("stale embedded '## Model statics' in project config: none")
    return "\n".join(out)


def main(argv=None):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="wf-model-budget",
        description="Emit the model budget picker's options from the shipped template.",
    )
    ap.add_argument("--root", default=".",
                    help="base for resolving relative --template/--config (the test harness "
                         "and audit invoke with the project root here)")
    ap.add_argument("--template", default=None,
                    help="path to org-config.template.md (default: co-located references/ copy)")
    ap.add_argument("--config", default=None,
                    help="path to the project's org-config.md (re-audit defaults + stale-section check)")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    # AN EMPTY --root IS A CALLER ERROR, NEVER A SILENT RETARGET TO CWD.
    # `${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}` is UNSET in the Bash tool — measured 2026-08-04 on
    # 2.1.221 — so every shipped `--root "${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}"` block expands to
    # `--root ""`. `os.path.abspath("")` is the CWD, a real directory, so an
    # is-it-a-directory guard passes. Here the empty value would silently resolve
    # `--template`/`--config` against a tree nobody named: the picker would either
    # fail claiming the TEMPLATE is unreadable — sending the caller to fix the wrong
    # end of the problem — or, worse, derive the pool from whatever `template.md`
    # happened to sit in the CWD. Refuse it and name the empty argument.
    _validate_grouping()
    if not (args.root or "").strip():
        _die("--root is empty. ${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR} is unset outside a hook; "
             "pass an explicit path.")

    def _resolve(p):
        # Absolute paths pass through; relative paths resolve against --root, so a
        # fixture copied to a temp tree finds its own frozen template.
        return p if os.path.isabs(p) else os.path.join(args.root, p)

    template_path = _resolve(args.template) if args.template else default_template_path()
    template_text = _read(template_path)
    statics = parse_statics(template_text)
    pool_ids = {m for m, _n, _l in statics}
    rec = merge_recommendations(statics, parse_out_of_pool_recs(template_text, pool_ids))
    wording = parse_wording(template_text)

    defaults, stale = {}, False
    config_path = _resolve(args.config) if args.config else None
    if config_path and os.path.exists(config_path):
        config_text = _read(config_path)
        defaults = parse_config_defaults(config_text)
        stale = config_has_stale_statics(config_text)

    sys.stdout.write(emit(statics, rec, defaults, stale, template_path, wording) + "\n")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
